## D. Documents downstream of the new results

- [ ] **D2. `x_L` convention inconsistent across remarks.** The solver's own
  `recap_edge` and the plateau-derived point (last grid point where `v'`
  still equals `1+kappa`) differ by exactly one grid cell at every
  `lambda_S`, confirmed by `verify_document_figures.py` (F6/F7) — CONFIRMED
  AGAIN on his own solve output 13 Aug (Octave 7.1.0): 1.030 vs 1.025 through
  1.105 vs 1.100, one cell apart at every point, not an artefact of my
  sandbox's Octave build. `rem:compstat`
  (CSL)'s boundary figures use the solver's edge; `rem:socn`'s `Psi(x_L)`
  figures use the plateau point. Both are defensible readings of "the
  trigger," but the document doesn't say which it means where, and the two
  remarks currently use different ones silently. Needs a stated convention,
  then a check that every other numerical remark referencing `x_L` agrees
  with it. DECISION NEEDED FROM HIM before this can be edited: which
  convention is canonical.
- [ ] **D3. TCSN and KCSN understate what's proved.** Both still describe
  `V''(x_L)>0` as resting on `rem:nonconcave` (LNC) and call it "a numerical
  finding." Proposition SOC now proves the weak inequality unconditionally
  and the strict one modulo a stated non-degeneracy condition — LNC is no
  longer load-bearing there (see SOCN). Small edit, deliberately left
  unmade pending the numerical run.

## A. Analytic gaps in the theory

- [ ] **A1/A2 (narrowed). `C¹` regularity of `v` at the recapitalisation
  trigger.** Both former items reduce to this single point, as suspected.
  Interior regularity is now settled (ITR): uniform ellipticity on
  compacts of `(1,∞)`, `C²` on the inaction region and `C³` away from
  `{R, x̄}` — and all three free boundaries sit clear of those kinks at the
  baseline. Verification is now conditional-proved (VER) on hypotheses
  (H1)–(H4), of which (H2)–(H4) hold and (H4) is enforced on every solve.
  What is left is hypothesis (H1) at one point: the smooth-fit principle
  at `x_L`. Standard for impulse control, asserted not proved here, and
  the mesh cannot presently resolve it. Closing it closes verification.

